      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:altLabel": [
        {
          "@value": "Seafood",
          "@language": "en"
        }
      ],
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "A seafood product is a vertebrate or invertibrate organism from an aquatic environment.",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000002"
      },
      "skos:relatedMatch": [
        {
          "@id": "http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/FOODON_00001046"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000033",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000033",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Crustacean",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "Crustaceans form a large, diverse arthropod taxon which includes such familiar animals as crabs, lobsters, crayfish, shrimp, krill, woodlice, and barnacles.",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000035"
      },
      "skos:relatedMatch": [
        {
          "@id": "http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/FOODON_03411374"
        },
        {
          "@id": "http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NCBITaxon_6657"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000034",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000034",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Fish",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "Fish are the gill-bearing aquatic craniate animals that lack limbs with digits. Most fish are ectothermic (\"cold-blooded\"), allowing their body temperatures to vary as ambient temperatures change, though some of the large active swimmers like white shark and tuna can hold a higher core temperature.",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000032"
      },
      "skos:relatedMatch": [
        {
          "@id": "http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/FOODON_03411222"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000035",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000035",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Shellfish",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "The term shellfish is used both broadly and specifically. For regulatory purposes it is often narrowly defined as filter-feeding molluscs such as clams, mussels, and oyster to the exclusion of crustaceans and all else. Although their shells may differ, all shellfish are invertebrates.",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000032"
      },
      "skos:relatedMatch": [
        {
          "@id": "http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/FOODON_03411433"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000036",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000036",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Collector Urchin",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:altLabel": [
        {
          "@value": "Hawaiian Sea Urchin",
          "@language": "en"
        }
      ],
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "A taxonomic concept used in AOM to identify Collector Urchin within the Urchins classification.",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_101017"
      },
      "skos:relatedMatch": [
        {
          "@id": "http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NCBITaxon_7673"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000037",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000037",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Mollusc",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:altLabel": [
        {
          "@value": "Mollusk",
          "@language": "en"
        }
      ],
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "Mollusca is a large phylum of invertebrate animals whose members are known as molluscs or mollusks. Molluscs are the largest marine phylum, comprising about 23% of all the named marine organisms. Numerous molluscs also live in freshwater and terrestrial habitats. [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mollusca]",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000035"
      },
      "skos:relatedMatch": [
        {
          "@id": "http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/FOODON_03412112"
        },
        {
          "@id": "http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NCBITaxon_6447"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000038",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000038",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Gastropod",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:altLabel": [
        {
          "@value": "Snail",
          "@language": "en"
        }
      ],
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "The Gastropoda or gastropods, more commonly known as snails and slugs, are a large taxonomic class within the phylum Mollusca. Gastropoda (previously known as univalves and sometimes spelled \"Gasteropoda\") are a major part of the phylum Mollusca, and are the most highly diversified class in the phylum, with 65,000 to 80,000 living snail and slug species. [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gastropoda] The taxonomic class *Gastropoda* also includes snails that live in different habitats, such as land snails, marine and freshwater snails.",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000037"
      },
      "skos:relatedMatch": [
        {
          "@id": "http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/FOODON_03412111"
        },
        {
          "@id": "http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NCBITaxon_6448"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000039",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000039",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Cephalapod",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:altLabel": [
        {
          "@value": "Cephalopod",
          "@language": "en"
        }
      ],
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "A cephalopod is any member of the molluscan class Cephalopoda such as a squid, octopus or nautilus. These exclusively marine animals are characterized by bilateral body symmetry, a prominent head, and a set of arms or tentacles modified from the primitive molluscan foot. Fishermen sometimes call them inkfish, referring to their common ability to squirt ink. [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cephalopod]",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000037"
      },
      "skos:relatedMatch": [
        {
          "@id": "http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/FOODON_03412116"
        },
        {
          "@id": "http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NCBITaxon_6605"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000040",
