      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "An experimental diet contains more manure than a control diet.",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000109"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000120",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000120",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Use of Supplements",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:altLabel": [
        {
          "@value": "use of dietary supplements",
          "@language": "en"
        },
        {
          "@value": "use of nutritional supplements",
          "@language": "en"
        },
        {
          "@value": "nutritional supplementation",
          "@language": "en"
        },
        {
          "@value": "dietary supplementation",
          "@language": "en"
        }
      ],
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "An experimental diet contains more dietary supplements (NCIT_C1505) than a control diet. According to the NCIT ontology these are \"Oral preparations containing dietary ingredient(s) intended to supplement the diet. Dietary ingredients include vitamins, minerals, herbs, amino acids, extracts and metabolites\". Supplements do not form a large proportion of the diet, their addition should have a minimal impact on the ratios of other ingredients in the diet and it's overall energy, protein and fibre content. The following are not supplements: 1) ingredients or products intended to treat diseases or medical conditions (e.g. drugs, vaccine or ectoparasite sprays); 2) growth promotors; and 3) immunomodulators.",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000109"
      },
      "skos:relatedMatch": [
        {
          "@id": "http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MAXO_0000106"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000121",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000121",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "vitamin supplementation",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "An experimental diet contains more vitamin (NCIT_C944) supplements than a control diet.",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000120"
      },
      "skos:relatedMatch": [
        {
          "@id": "http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MAXO_0001129"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000122",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000122",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "mineral supplementation",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "An experimental diet contains more mineral supplements than a control diet.",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000120"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000123",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000123",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "herb or extract supplementation",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "An experimental diet contains more herb or herb extract (FOODON_03420228) supplements than a control diet. Herb or extract supplements are natural products derived from plants that are added to livestock feed to provide specific health benefits (human validated ChatGPT). These supplements can be in the form of dried or fresh herbs, as well as concentrated extracts, tinctures, or essential oils (human validated ChatGPT). Garlic, oregano, thyme, rosemary, and echinacea extracts are common herb supplements used in livestock (human validated ChatGPT).",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000120"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000124",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000124",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "amino acid supplementation",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "An experimental diet contains more amino acid (CHEBI_33709) supplements than a control diet.",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000120"
      },
      "skos:relatedMatch": [
        {
          "@id": "http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MAXO_0010020"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000125",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000125",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "enzyme supplementation",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "An experimental diet contains more enzyme supplements than a control diet. These are plant or animal enzymes used to facilitate the digestive process.",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000120"
      },
      "skos:relatedMatch": [
        {
          "@id": "http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/FOODON_03401311"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000126",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000126",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "protease supplementation",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "An experimental diet contains more protease enzyme (BAO_0000652) supplements than a control diet. Proteases are enzymes that break down proteins into smaller peptides and amino acids, which are more easily absorbed and utilized by the animal. Proteases are commonly used in to improve protein digestion and utilization (human validated ChatGPT).",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000125"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000127",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000127",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "carbohydrase supplementation",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "An experimental diet contains more carbohydrases enzyme supplements than a control diet. Carbohydrases are enzymes that break down complex carbohydrates such as starches, cellulose, and hemicellulose into simpler sugars such as glucose, which can be used for energy by the animal (human validated ChatGPT). Carbohydrases are commonly used in to improve feed efficiency and reduce the amount of undigested feed in manure (human validated ChatGPT).",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000125"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000128",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000128",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "phytase supplementation",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "An experimental diet contains more phytase enzyme supplements than a control diet. Phytases are enzymes that break down phytic acid, a form of phosphorus that is bound to plant proteins and unavailable to the animal (human validated ChatGPT). By breaking down phytic acid, phytases release more available phosphorus for absorption by the animal (human validated ChatGPT). Phytases are commonly used to improve phosphorus utilization and reduce phosphorus excretion into the environment (human validated ChatGPT).",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000125"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000129",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000129",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "lipase supplementation",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "An experimental diet contains more lipase enzyme (TXPO_0002283) supplements than a control diet. Lipases are enzymes that break down fats and oils into fatty acids and glycerol, which can be used for energy by the animal (human validated ChatGPT). Lipases are commonly used in poultry and swine diets to improve fat digestion and utilization (human validated ChatGPT).",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000125"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000130",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000130",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "probiotic supplementation",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:altLabel": [
        {
          "@value": "probiotic or bacteria supplement",
          "@language": "en"
        }
      ],
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "An experimental diet contains more probiotic supplements than a control diet. Probiotics are live microorganisms thought to be healthy for the host organism. According to the currently adopted definition by FAO/WHO, probiotics are selected \"live microorganisms which when administered in adequate amounts confer a health benefit on the host\".[FAO/WHO (2001) Health and Nutritional Properties of Probiotics in Food including Powder Milk with Live Lactic Acid Bacteria. Report of a Joint FAO/WHO Expert Consultation on Evaluation of Health and Nutritional Properties of Probiotics in Food Including Powder Milk with Live Lactic Acid Bacteria] Lactic acid bacteria (LAB) and bifidobacteria are the most common types of microbes used as probiotics; but also certain yeasts and bacilli are available. [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Probiotics]",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000120"
      },
      "skos:relatedMatch": [
        {
          "@id": "http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/FOODON_03401308"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000131",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000131",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "prebiotic supplementation",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "An experimental diet contains more prebiotic supplements than a control diet. Prebiotics are non-digestible fibers that promote the growth of beneficial bacteria in the gut, helping to improve digestion and immune function (human validated ChatGPT).",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000120"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000132",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000132",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "essential fatty acid supplementation",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "An experimental diet contains more essential fatty acid supplements than a control diet. Essential fatty acids include omega-3 (CHEBI_25681) amd omega-6 (CHEBI_36009).",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000120"
      },
      "skos:relatedMatch": [
        {
          "@id": "http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MAXO_0010024"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000133",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000133",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "organic acid supplementation",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "An experimental diet contains more organic acid supplements than a control diet. Organic acids work in livestock feeds by reducing the pH of the gut, which creates an environment that is less hospitable to harmful bacteria (human validated ChatGPT). In addition to reducing pH, organic acids can also disrupt the cell membranes of bacteria, causing them to leak and die (human validated ChatGPT).",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000120"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000134",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000134",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "urea supplementation",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "An experimental diet contains more urea supplement than a control diet.  Urea is a high-nitrogen compound that can be converted by rumen microbes into usable protein for the animal. Urea treatment is a cost-effective way to increase the protein content of low-quality forage and other feed sources, particularly in areas where high-quality protein supplements are not readily available (human validated ChatGPT).",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000120"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000135",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000135",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Addition",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "A diet is modified by adding new ingredients to a control diet.   A simple case is grazing only compared to grazing plus additional feed.  Another case is adding a new ingredient to a control feed which is not already included in that control.  In experiments where feed provision is limited the total amount of feed provided to an animal should increase as a result of feed addition. In experiments where feed is provided ad libitum or to satiation the proximate composition the changed diet should show the proportion of ingredients all decline the same amount relative to the control. For example, a control diet is 2 kg hay (proportion = 0.66) and 1 kg maize (0.33) and an experimental diet adds 3 kg of cotton seed cake (3/6 = 0.5) so hay becomes (2/6= 0.33), maize becomes (1/6 =0.167), in this case the control ingredients have decreased the same relative amount (50%). If some diet items decrease relatively more than others, this may indicate substitution.",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000109"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000136",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000136",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Substitution",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "In substitution-based experiments, certain ingredients in a control diet are replaced with different ingredients in an experimental diet. Typically, both the control and experimental diets are provided in equal weights or with equivalent amounts of energy and/or protein.",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000109"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000137",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000137",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Fed Same Weight",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "The diets being compared are fed at the same rate (for example both control and experimental diets are fed at 1 kg/animal/day).",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000109"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000138",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000138",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Fed Same Energy",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "The diets being compared are designed to fed to provide a similar amount of energy.",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000109"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000139",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000139",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Fed Same Protein",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "The diets being compared are fed to provide a similar amount of protein.",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000109"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000140",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000140",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Control",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "A control or reference diet serves as the standard or baseline nutritional plan against which the effects of modified or experimental diets are compared. This diet typically reflects the current or commonly used feeding practices for a specific animal species, growth stage, or production goal. (HV GPT4)",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000109"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000141",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000141",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "On-farm",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "A diet or ingredient produced on-farm refers to feed materials that are cultivated, harvested, or otherwise obtained within the same agricultural system where the livestock consuming them are raised.",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_100992"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000142",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000142",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Purchased",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "Purchased feeds refer to diets or ingredients that are obtained from external sources rather than being produced on-farm. These feed materials are typically bought from suppliers or manufacturers and are used to supplement or provide nutrition to livestock in the absence of sufficient on-farm feed resources.",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_100992"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000143",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000143",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Feed Processing",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "The application of feed transformation techniques, such as grinding, pelleting, extrusion, or ensiling, to modify the physical or chemical properties of raw feed materials in a diet. (HV GPT4)",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000109"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000144",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000144",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Dehydration Processing",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "Dehydration processing refers to the removal of moisture from raw feed materials to enhance preservation, handling, and storage properties. This process can occur through natural methods, such as sun or air drying, or artificial methods, including oven or forced-air drying. Dehydration processing focuses on reducing the moisture content in feedstuffs, distinguishing it from mechanical, biological, chemical, or thermal methods. (HC GPT4)",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000143"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000145",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000145",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Biological Processing",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "Biological processing refers to the utilization of microorganisms, such as bacteria or fungi, to break down and transform feed materials, enhancing their nutritional value, digestibility, and preservation properties. Biological processes, including fermentation, rely on the metabolic activities of microorganisms to produce organic acids, enzymes, or other bioactive compounds that modify feed composition and characteristics, distinguishing biological processing from mechanical, chemical, or thermal methods.",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000143"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000146",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000146",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Enzyme Treatment",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "Enzyme treatment is a biological feed processing technique that involves incorporating biologically derived catalysts, such as proteases, amylases, or cellulases, into raw feed materials before consumption by animals. These enzymes, typically produced by microorganisms or sourced from plants, act on specific substrates to break down complex molecules into simpler, more digestible components, enhancing nutrient availability and overall feed quality. This process is distinct from enzyme supplementation, where enzymes are added to the feed and act within the animal's digestive system after ingestion. (HV GPT4)",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000145"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000147",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000147",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Fermentation",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "Feed fermentation is a biological process of preserving animal feeds, such as grains or forage, by allowing them to undergo controlled microbial breakdown. This process can occur naturally, or it can be initiated by adding specific microbial cultures. During fermentation, the microorganisms convert some of the feed's carbohydrates and sugars into organic acids, such as lactic acid, which lower the pH and inhibit the growth of harmful bacteria. The resulting fermented feed has a higher concentration of beneficial nutrients, such as vitamins and amino acids, and is more easily digestible by livestock. (human validated chatGPT).",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000145"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000148",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000148",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Thermal Processing",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "Livestock feed thermal processing refers to the application of heat to raw feed materials in order to alter properties such as nutrient availability, digestibility, and safety for animal consumption. Methods include roasting, steam treatment, and extrusion, where heat exposure drives the changes in feed properties, distinguishing thermal processing from mechanical, chemical, or biological methods. (HV GPT4)",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000143"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000149",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000149",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Chemical Processing",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "Chemical processing refers to the deliberate application of chemical agents or reactions to raw feed materials in order to enhance nutrient availability, digestibility, and safety for animal consumption. These processes include, but are not limited to, acid or alkali treatment, and ammoniation, which modify the chemical composition, molecular structure, or preservation properties of feedstuffs, ultimately improving the overall efficiency of animal production systems. Chemical processing does not include enzymes, these are considered to  be biological processing. (HV GPT4)",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000143"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000150",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000150",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Mechanical Processing",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "Mechnical processing manipulates the physical structure of raw feed materials in order to improve digestibility, nutrient availability, and feed handling properties. Key processes include grinding, mixing, pelleting, and extrusion, which alter particle size, texture, and shape to optimize feed intake and nutrient utilization by animals. (HV GPT4)",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000143"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000151",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000151",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Herd Management",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "Management practices that oversee the health and breeding of domesticated animals (human validated BARD).",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000107"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000152",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000152",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Genetic Improvement",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:altLabel": [
        {
          "@value": "Herd Improvement",
          "@language": "en"
        }
      ],
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "The selection and/or breeding of animals with desirable traits to improve outcomes.",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000151"
      },
      "skos:relatedMatch": [
        {
          "@id": "http://aims.fao.org/aos/agrovoc/c_d25514a9"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000153",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000153",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Unimproved Breed",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "A local or otherwise unimproved animal breed.",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000152"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000154",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000154",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Improved Breeds",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "The use of improved animal breeds, these are animals bred for specific traits usually productivity (typically breeds exotic to Africa like Friesen, Holstein or Jersey cows).",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000152"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000155",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000155",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Hybridization or Cross Breeding",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "The use of animal cross breeds produced by the mating (hybridization) of individuals of different breeds, varieties, species, or genera. [http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NCIT_C128790]",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000152"
      },
      "skos:relatedMatch": [
        {
          "@id": "http://aims.fao.org/aos/agrovoc/c_3706"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000156",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000156",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Heat Tolerance",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "The use of improved animal breeds bred for heat tolerance.",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000154"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000157",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000157",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Manure Management",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "Livestock manure management is the collection, storage, treatment, and utilization of animal waste  (human validated BARD).",
