      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000811",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000811",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Herb or Extract",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "An AOM controlled category for “Herb or Extract” within “Feed Ingredient”. Used to classify feed records at this scope; narrower material characteristics require separate assertions.",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_100850"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000817",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000817",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Ammonia Treatment",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "Ammonia treatment is a feed processing technique that involves the application of ammonia (NH3) or ammonium hydroxide (NH4OH) to raw feed materials, primarily to enhance the digestibility of fibrous components and provide an additional source of non-protein nitrogen for ruminant animals. By solubilizing hemicellulose, lignin, and other complex carbohydrates, this alkaline treatment improves the overall nutritional value of fibrous feedstuffs. Furthermore, rumen microbes can utilize the non-protein nitrogen to synthesize microbial protein, contributing to the nutritional benefits of ammonia-treated feeds. (HV GPT4)",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000819"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000818",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000818",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Acid Treatment",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "Acid treatment is a feed processing technique that involves the application of acidic substances to raw feed materials, aiming to improve nutrient availability, digestibility, and preservation properties. This process typically uses organic or inorganic acids, such as phosphoric, sulfuric, or citric acid, which can break down complex molecules, lower pH, and inhibit microbial growth. (HV GPT4)",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_100991"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000819",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000819",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Alkali Treatment",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "Alkali treatment is an feed processing technique that involves the application of alkaline substances to raw feed materials, primarily to enhance the digestibility of fibrous components. This process typically uses alkaline agents, such as sodium hydroxide, potassium hydroxide, or ammonia, which can solubilize hemicellulose, lignin, and other complex carbohydrates, improving the overall nutritional value of fibrous feedstuffs. (HV GPT4)",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_100991"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000820",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000820",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Enzyme Treatment",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "Enzyme treatment is a biological feed processing technique that involves incorporating biologically derived catalysts, such as proteases, amylases, or cellulases, into raw feed materials before consumption by animals. These enzymes, typically produced by microorganisms or sourced from plants, act on specific substrates to break down complex molecules into simpler, more digestible components, enhancing nutrient availability and overall feed quality. This process is distinct from enzyme supplementation, where enzymes are added to the feed and act within the animal's digestive system after ingestion. (HV GPT4)",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_100990"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000821",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000821",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Cellulase Treatment",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "Cellulase treatment is a feed processing method that involves applying cellulase enzymes to raw feed materials before animal consumption. This enzymatic process breaks down cellulose present in plant-based feeds into simpler, more digestible components, thereby improving nutrient availability and overall feed quality. (HV GPT4)",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_006824"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000822",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000822",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Hemicellulase Treatment",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "Hemicellulase treatment: Hemicellulase treatment is a feed processing technique that incorporates hemicellulase enzymes into raw feed materials prior to ingestion by animals. These enzymes target hemicellulose, a complex carbohydrate present in plant cell walls, breaking it down into simpler components to enhance nutrient absorption and feed value. (HV GPT4)",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_006824"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000823",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000823",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Protease Treatment",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "Protease treatment: Protease treatment is a method of feed processing that utilizes protease enzymes to act on raw feed materials before they are consumed by animals. Proteases target protein molecules, breaking them into smaller peptides and amino acids, which increases protein digestibility and nutritional value of the feed. (HV GPT4)",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000820"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000824",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000824",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Amylase Treatment",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "Amylase treatment: Amylase treatment is a feed processing approach that involves adding amylase enzymes to raw feed materials before animal consumption. Amylases break down complex carbohydrates, such as starch, into simpler sugars, which enhances energy availability and overall feed efficiency. (HV GPT4)",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000820"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000825",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000825",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Phytase Treatment",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "Phytase treatment: Phytase treatment is a feed processing technique that incorporates phytase enzymes into raw feed materials prior to animal ingestion. Phytases target phytic acid, an anti-nutritional factor present in plant-based feeds, releasing bound phosphorus and other minerals for improved bioavailability, enhancing the nutritional value of the feed. (HV GPT4)",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000820"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000826",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000826",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Thermal Process",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:altLabel": [
        {
          "@value": "Feed Heating",
          "@language": "en"
        }
      ],
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "The process of applying heat to animal feed, typically through steam or direct heating, to improve its digestibility, increase nutrient availability, and reduce the presence of harmful microorganisms. This process may involve the use of various equipment, such as ovens, boilers, heaters, and dryers, and is often used in commercial feed production. (human validated chatGPT)",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000845"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000827",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000827",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Boiling",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "Feed boiling refers to the process of heating animal feed in water or steam for a specific period of time to improve its digestibility and nutritional value by reducing anti-nutritional factors, such as toxins and enzyme inhibitors, and by increasing the availability of nutrients to the animal. (Human validated chatGPT).",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000826"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000828",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000828",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Direct Boiling",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "Direct boiling involves placing the feed directly in boiling water for a certain period of time to improve its digestibility and reduce anti-nutritional factors. This process is typically used for small-scale feed processing, such as for backyard poultry or pigs. (Human validated chatGPT).",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000827"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000829",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000829",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Indirect Boiling",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:altLabel": [
        {
          "@value": "Steaming",
          "@language": "en"
        },
        {
          "@value": "Steamed Feed",
          "@language": "en"
        }
      ],
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "Indirect boiling involves using steam to indirectly heat the feed, either in a steam chamber or through the use of a steam jacketed kettle or cooker. Indirect boiling is typically used for larger-scale feed processing, such as in commercial feed mills. The steam provides gentle heating to the feed, improving its digestibility and reducing the risk of overcooking or damaging the feed. (Human validated chatGPT)",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000827"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000830",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000830",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Fermentation",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "Feed fermentation is a process of preserving animal feeds, such as grains or forage, by allowing them to undergo controlled microbial breakdown. This process can occur naturally, or it can be initiated by adding specific microbial cultures. During fermentation, the microorganisms convert some of the feed's carbohydrates and sugars into organic acids, such as lactic acid, which lower the pH and inhibit the growth of harmful bacteria. The resulting fermented feed has a higher concentration of beneficial nutrients, such as vitamins and amino acids, and is more easily digestible by livestock. (human validated chatGPT).",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_100990"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000831",
      "@type": [
        "skos:Concept",
        "aom:ProcessingMethod"
      ],
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000831",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Ensiling",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:altLabel": [
        {
          "@value": "Ensilation",
          "@language": "en"
        },
        {
          "@value": "Silage Process",
          "@language": "en"
        },
        {
          "@value": "Ensiled",
          "@language": "en"
        }
      ],
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "Ensilation is a specific form of fermentation to preserve forage by packing it tightly into a silo or other airtight container and allowing it to ferment. This fermentation process creates an anaerobic environment that preserves the feed and enhances its nutritional quality by increasing the concentration of organic acids and decreasing the pH. The resulting ensiled feed can be stored for extended periods of time and used as a source of nutrition for livestock. (human validated chatGPT)",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000830"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000832",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000832",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Roasting",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "Roasting refers to the process of heating animal feed to high temperatures, typically in a dry heat environment. The heat causes chemical reactions that break down complex carbohydrates and proteins, making them more easily digestible by the animal. Roasting may also reduce the presence of anti-nutritional factors and harmful microorganisms in the feed. (human validated chatGPT)",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000826"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000833",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000833",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Extrusion",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "Extrusion is a feed processing technique that involves forcing raw feed materials through a shaped die under high pressure and temperature conditions, resulting in a continuous, uniformly shaped product. This process can alter the physical and chemical properties of feed materials, improving digestibility, nutrient availability, and palatability. Extrusion is both a thermal and mechanical process. (HV GPT4)",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000826"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000834",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000834",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Chopping",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "Chopping is a feed processing technique that involves the mechanical cutting or shearing of raw feed materials into smaller, uniform pieces. This process often employs choppers, knives, or cutting mills to facilitate improved handling, mixing, and feed intake characteristics. (HV GPT4)",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000837"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000835",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000835",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Crushing",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "Crushing is a feed processing technique that involves the mechanical reduction of particle size in raw feed materials through the application of compressive force. This process typically uses crushers or roller mills, resulting in the rupture of cell structures and the release of nutrients, facilitating improved digestibility and nutrient availability. (HV GPT4)",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000837"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000836",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000836",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Grinding",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "Grinding, usually involves the use of abrasive surfaces, such as grinding wheels or hammer mills, to reduce the size of feed particles through friction and impact. Grinding generally results in a more consistent and fine particle size compared to milling. (HV GPT4)",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000837"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000837",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000837",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Mechanical Process",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "A mechanical process refers to the physical manipulation of raw feed materials using machinery in order to improve digestibility, nutrient availability, and feed handling properties, ultimately enhancing the overall efficiency of animal production systems. Key processes include grinding, mixing, pelleting, and extrusion, which alter particle size, texture, and shape to optimize feed intake and nutrient utilization by animals. (HV GPT4)",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000845"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000838",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000838",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Milling",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "Milling typically refers to the process of using rotary cutters or millstones to grind, crush, or shear feed materials into smaller particles. Milling can produce a wide range of particle sizes and is often used for producing flour or meal from grains and other feedstuffs.  (HV GPT4)",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000837"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000839",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000839",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Hammer Milling",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "Hammer milling is a mechanical feed processing technique that involves the use of rotating hammers to grind, crush, or pulverize raw feed materials into smaller particles. The process employs a high-speed rotor equipped with a series of hammers that impact the feed materials, breaking them apart and reducing their size. Hammer milling improves feed handling, mixing, and digestibility, while promoting uniform particle size distribution. (HV GPT4)",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000836"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000840",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000840",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Pelleting",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "Pelleting is a feed processing technique that involves the compression of raw feed materials into small, dense, cylindrical shapes called pellets. This process typically involves a combination of grinding, mixing, and conditioning, followed by the use of a pellet mill to create uniform pellets. Pelleting can improve feed handling, storage, and intake, while reducing waste and enhancing nutrient utilization. (HV GPT4)",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000837"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000841",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000841",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Extrusion",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "Extrusion is a feed processing technique that involves forcing raw feed materials through a shaped die under high pressure and temperature conditions, resulting in a continuous, uniformly shaped product. This process can alter the physical and chemical properties of feed materials, improving digestibility, nutrient availability, and palatability. Extrusion is both a thermal and mechanical process. (HV GPT4)",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000837"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000842",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000842",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Dehydration Process",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "Dehydration processing refers to the removal of moisture from raw feed materials to enhance preservation, handling, and storage properties. This process can occur through natural methods, such as sun or air drying, or artificial methods, including oven or forced-air drying. Dehydration processing focuses on reducing the moisture content in feedstuffs, distinguishing it from mechanical, biological, chemical, or thermal methods.(HV GPT4)",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000845"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000843",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000843",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Drying",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "Drying is a feed processing technique that involves the removal of moisture from raw feed materials to enhance preservation, handling, and storage properties. Drying can be achieved through natural methods, such as sun or air drying, or artificial methods, including oven or forced-air drying. (HV GPT4)",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000842"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000844",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000844",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Wilting",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "Wilting is a feed processing technique that involves partial dehydration of raw feed materials, typically through natural methods like sun or air exposure. The process reduces moisture content, which can improve preservation, handling, and storage properties, particularly for forage or silage production. (HV GPT4)",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000842"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000845",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:scheme:livestock-v2"
      },
      "skos:notation": "AOM_000845",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Feed Process",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "era:conceptType": "legacy_aom_concept",
      "era:status": "unknown",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "A livestock feed process is a specific technique or method applied to raw feed materials to alter their physical, chemical, or biological properties, with the goal of improving palatability, nutrient availability, and overall feed quality for animals. (HV GPT4)",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "urn:era-aom:livestock:AOM_000328"
      }
    },
